* text=auto eol=crlf
# These are all bash scripts and should use lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf

# The icon corpus goes in and out of git byte for byte. Under the eol=crlf rule above git would
# see 3,894 text files, store them with LF and check them out with CRLF -- and mkpack.py copies an
# .svg into the pack verbatim, so a clone would build a pack that differs from this one by however
# many bytes got rewritten, and every icon offset after the first of them would move. It is 12 of
# the 3,894 that contain a newline at all, which is exactly the sort of margin that makes a bug
# like this survive: the pack would be 12 bytes off and 3,882 icons would look fine.
# LICENSE.txt is the authors' own licence statement and is kept as they wrote it for the same
# reason. README.md is ours and follows the repo convention.
assets/icons/**/*.svg -text
assets/icons/LICENSE.txt -text

src/libs/** linguist-vendored
assets/icons/** linguist-vendored

# Scripts with a shebang, run directly as well as through an interpreter. Under the eol=crlf rule
# above the first line becomes "#!/usr/bin/env python3\r", and the kernel looks for an interpreter
# with a carriage return in its name -- which fails as "Error 127", command not found, from a make
# rule that names the script and looks for all the world like a missing file. The Makefile invokes
# this one as `python3 tools/...` so the build does not depend on the bit, and it is still marked
# LF so that running it by hand works on a fresh clone.
tools/mkcharset-latin.py text eol=lf
